nm_h042","cnm_h039"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:810ed84716bbeac499b11952"],"answerCheck":{"adjudication":"Root independently inspected the displayed stem, choices and mathematical counterexamples or ordinary task convention.","derivedAnswer":"B for positive terms with the displayed quotient defined; no universal formula as printed","reason":"For nonzero geometric terms the reversed-pair product K gives S=K*Sprime and P^2=K^n. Positive terms select the keyed positive value. However (-1,-1,-1) gives S=Sprime=-3 and product-1, while B gives+1. Also (1,-1) has nonzero reciprocals but S=Sprime=0, so B is undefined. The printed task does not require positive terms or nonzero reciprocal sum. Preserve B with its conditions and recognize these counterexamples.","recordedAnswer":"B","status":"confirmed_ambiguous"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h039"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":["The question does not state that the terms are positive; taking the positive square-root branch can lose the sign of P.","Nonzero terms do not ensure S' is nonzero, so S/S' need not be defined.","Positivity is not stated. For odd n, the displayed fractional-power formula can lose the sign of P.","Nonzero terms do not ensure S′ ≠ 0; zero-sum progressions can make S/S′ undefined.","The unconditional identities S = CS′ and P² = C^n remain valid, but they do not establish an unconditional listed answer.","confirmed_answer_ambiguity"],"questionId":"1971_AMC_12_Q33","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"reverse-reciprocal-pairing","label":"Pair terms with reversed reciprocals","methodDirectNodeIds":{"reverse-reciprocal-pairing":["cnm_160","cnm_h039"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_160","cnm_h039"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"geometric-sum-formulas","label":"Compare geometric sum formulas","methodDirectNodeIds":{"geometric-sum-formulas":["cnm_h210","cnm_h039"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h210","cnm_h039"]},{"contextNodeIds":["c