2a^2/√S. Dividing by S gives 2a^2/S^(3/2).","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h039"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":["For real a and x, the original expression requires (a,x)≠(0,0).","For real parameters the original expression excludes (a,x)=(0,0); simplification preserves that restriction."],"questionId":"1953_AMC_12_Q07","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_167","cnm_168","cnm_h039"],"id":"common-radical-denominator","label":"Combine the numerator first","methodDirectNodeIds":{"common-radical-denominator":["cnm_173"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_173","cnm_167","cnm_168","cnm_h039"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:810ed84716bbeac499b11952"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, 1","reason":"The common ratio is 2^(1/3-1/2)=2^(-1/6). The fourth term is 2^(1/6)·2^(-1/6)=1.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h039"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1961_AMC_12_Q12","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_164"],"id":"common-ratio","label":"Find the common ratio","methodDirectNodeIds":{"common-ratio":["cnm_h207","cnm_h039","math:real-exponent-laws"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h207","cnm_h039","math:real-exponent-laws","cnm_164"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:810ed84716bbeac499b11952"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, -21","reason":"The term indexed by k is (-1)^k C(7,k)a^(7-3k/2). Exponent -1/2 requires k = 5, giving coefficient -C(7,5) = -21.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h039"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1963_AMC_12_Q09","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h240","cnm_h039","cnm_h237"],"id":"match-binomial-term-exponent","label":"Find the required binomial term","methodDirectNodeIds":{"match-binomial-term-exponent":["cnm_h241"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h241","cnm_h240","cnm_h039","cnm_h237"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:810ed84716bbeac499b11952"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A: 1/[2(1-2^(-1/32))]","reason":"Let t = 2^(-1/32). Multiplying the product by 1-t gives 1-t^32 = 1/2. Since t is not 1, s = 1/[2(1-t)].","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h039"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1971_AMC_12_Q32","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_147","cnm_h039","math:real-exponent-laws"],"id":"successive-differences-of-squares","label":"Collapse the product by differences of squares","methodDirectNodeIds":{"successive-differences-of-squares":["math:telescoping"]},"nodeIds":["math:telescoping","cnm_147","cnm_h039","math:real-exponent-laws"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:810ed84716bbeac499b11952"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 55/3","reason":"If log_8 n=p/q, then n^q=2^(3p), so n must be a power of 2. The eligible arguments are 2^j for 0≤j≤10. Their contributions sum to (0+1+...+10)/3=55/3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h039"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1997_AMC_12_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h039","cnm_h204"],"id":"rational-log-arguments","label":"Identify the arguments with rational logarithms","methodDirectNodeIds":{"rational-log-arguments":["cnm_h043","math:prime-factorization"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h043","math:prime-factorization","cnm_h039","cnm_h204"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:810ed84716bbeac499b11952"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B (270)","reason":"Equal differences of logarithms give a common ratio r between successive arguments. Thus r^4=1250/162=625/81=(5/3)^4. Since r is positive, r=5/3 and x=162(5/3)=270.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h039"],"coverag