n=7 its exponent is 64/7<19/2, so the product is less than 512sqrt(2)<1000. At n=9 its exponent is 100/7>10, so it exceeds 1024. Monotonicity makes 9 the smallest eligible odd integer.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h039"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1976_AMC_12_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h039","cnm_079","cnm_p156"],"id":"sum-exponents-and-bound","label":"Combine the powers and compare neighboring odd values","methodDirectNodeIds":{"sum-exponents-and-bound":["cnm_h204","math:real-exponent-laws","cnm_h042"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h204","math:real-exponent-laws","cnm_h042","cnm_h039","cnm_079","cnm_p156"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"alignmentIds":["alignment:810ed84716bbeac499b11952"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A: 3/4","reason":"The sixth root of 27 is sqrt(3), while sqrt(6 3/4) = 3sqrt(3)/2. Their difference is -sqrt(3)/2, whose square is 3/4.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h039"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1986_AMC_12_Q05","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_168","cnm_169","cnm_170","cnm_172"],"id":"1986_AMC_12_Q05_m1","label":"Simplify the roots before squaring","methodDirectNodeIds":{"1986_AMC_12_Q05_m1":["cnm_173","cnm_h039"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_173","cnm_h039","cnm_168","cnm_169","cnm_170","cnm_172"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"alignmentIds":["alignment:810ed84716bbeac499b11952"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, 17","reason":"The exponents satisfy b_0=0, b_1=1 and b_n=b_(n−1)+2b_(n−2), giving b_n=(2^n−(−1)^n)/3. Their sum is S_k=(2^(k+2)−3−(−1)^k)/6. The order of 2 modulo 19 is 18, so 19 divides S_k first at odd k=17 or even k=18. Therefore the smallest is 17; indeed S_17=87381=19·4599, making the product 2^4599.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h039"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","