","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h039"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2008_AMC_12A_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h097","cnm_h098","cnm_072","cnm_p148","cnm_h039"],"id":"translated-fourth-power","label":"Translate the roots and use quarter-turns","methodDirectNodeIds":{"translated-fourth-power":["math:roots-of-unity","cnm_h102","cnm_152"]},"nodeIds":["math:roots-of-unity","cnm_h102","cnm_152","cnm_h097","cnm_h098","cnm_072","cnm_p148","cnm_h039"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"alignmentIds":["alignment:810ed84716bbeac499b11952"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 10∜8","reason":"Let r = AB/H. The upright area-36 and area-4 rectangles have widths 6/√r and 2/√r. Together they span the area-32 rectangle, whose width is √(32r). Thus 8/√r = √(32r), giving r = √2. The areas sum to 200, so AB = √(200√2) = 10∜8.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h039"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2024_AMC_10A_Q16","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_268","cnm_169","cnm_170"],"id":"2024_AMC_10A_Q16_m1","label":"Find the common side ratio","methodDirectNodeIds":{"2024_AMC_10A_Q16_m1":["cnm_270","cnm_p089","cnm_p150","cnm_220","cnm_h039"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_270","cnm_p089","cnm_p150","cnm_220","cnm_h039","cnm_268","cnm_169","cnm_170"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":8,"alignmentIds":["alignment:810ed84716bbeac499b11952"],"answerCheck":{"adjudication":"Root independently inspected the displayed stem, choices and mathematical counterexamples or ordinary task convention.","derivedAnswer":"a=3^(1/6) or a=3^(-1/6); only the first is listed as A","reason":"A square has side6. Write A=(X,h), B=(Y,h), C=(Y,h+delta*6), delta=+1 or-1. Equal logarithmic heights imply X=Y^2; |Y^2-Y|=6 and Y>0 force Y=3,X=9. Then log_a(3)=delta*6. Both reciprocal bases satisfy all displayed conditions, with explicit squares at heights12,18 or-12,-18.